The '''Province of Lacashe''' (1516–1789) was the southernmost and wealthiest of the three provinces comprising [[New Burawa]] ({{lang|so|Burawi Cusub}}), the [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawi]] colonial possession on the eastern seaboard of [[Abyala]]. Founded in 1516 by the navigator [[Guled Badweyn]] as the first Burawi settlement on the continent, Lacashe developed into the centre of the Burawi plantation economy, becoming the largest exporter of cotton in the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] basin by the mid-18th century.

The province was characterised by its plantation economy worked by enslaved [[Kaftia]]n labourers, a hierarchical society organised around clan-based planter families, and a complex relationship with the metropolitan government in [[Samara]]. Following the [[Burawi Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709, Lacashe was granted autonomy as a self-governing parliamentary republic within the Burawi sphere, though foreign affairs remained under metropolitan control. In 1789, when the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] passed the [[Abolition of Slavery Act 1789]], the planter-controlled Parliament of Lacashe declared independence rather than accept emancipation. The resulting independent state maintained chattel slavery until the [[War of Liberation (1853–1856)|War of Liberation]], when a coalition of powers including Burawa, [[Volisania|Volisania]], [[Balisca]], and [[Asikyira]] invaded and abolished the institution by force.

== Etymology ==

The name '''Lacashe''' derives from the [[Burawi language|Burawi]] term given to the province surrounding the original settlement. The precise etymology is uncertain; scholarly interpretations include derivation from {{lang|so|lacaag}} ("to set anchor" or "to make landfall") or from an indigenous [[Tapuya Confederacy|Tapuya]] term for the coastal region. The original settlement was named '''[[Guledga]]''' after the colony's founder [[Guled Badweyn]]; by the early 17th century, the province had become known as Lacashe while the city retained the name Guledga.

The term was used throughout the colonial period to refer to the southernmost of the three Burawi Abyalan provinces. Following independence in 1789, the name was retained for the sovereign state, while the capital city remained Guledga.

== Geography ==

[[File:Guledga_harbour_1600.jpg|thumb|250px|The harbour of Guledga, the province's primary port and administrative centre, circa 1600]]

Lacashe occupied a coastal strip along the southeastern portion of the [[Abyala]]n continent, extending from the shoreline of the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] westward to the foothills of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]]. The terrain comprised primarily hills, plains, and marshlands, with fertile alluvial soils deposited by rivers descending from the interior mountains. The relatively flat topography and tropical climate, with high temperatures and seasonal rainfall, made the province ideal for plantation agriculture.

The provincial boundaries were not precisely delineated during the early colonial period but became formalised through administrative practice. The province extended inland approximately 80 to 120 kilometres, encompassing the entire coastal plain between the southern terminus of the mountain range and the ocean. The northern boundary with the province of [[Bindorama]] was established at a river mouth approximately 200 kilometres north of the capital.

The provincial and colonial capital, [[Guledga]], was situated at the original landing site on a natural harbour protected by a headland. The city grew into the largest port in Burawi Abyala and the seat of the colonial bishopric.

== History ==

=== Founding and early settlement (1516–1560s) ===
{{main|Colonial Expansion of Burawa}}

The colony was established in 1516 by the navigator [[Guled Badweyn]], who had been commissioned by [[Dalgayan III]] to explore the eastern coast of [[Abyala]]. The initial settlement comprised a fortified trading post, a harbour, a [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] mission, and supporting agricultural land. The site was chosen for its strategic position at the margin of two indigenous political systems: the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] to the west in the interior lowlands, and the coastal zone inhabited by Tapuya communities who maintained fishing and gathering settlements along the shore.

The early settler population was heterogeneous. Soldiers garrisoned the fortifications; farmers cleared land for cultivation; merchants established trading houses; [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] missionaries operated parishes and schools. The crown also transported convicts to the colony, where they served sentences of forced labour before receiving land grants. Relations with the indigenous coastal peoples were initially commercial, with settlers trading manufactured goods for food and local knowledge. As plantation agriculture expanded, however, these relationships deteriorated into conflict over land and resources.

The colonial economy in the early decades was oriented toward the extraction of [[gold]] from alluvial deposits in the foothills and the cultivation of subsistence crops. By the 1540s, plantation agriculture had begun to shift toward cash crops — initially [[tobacco]] and [[cotton]] — for export to [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] markets. The demand for labour on these plantations drove the expansion of the [[Kaftian slave trade]], which would become the defining feature of Lacashe's economy.

=== Expansion and consolidation (1560s–1709) ===

The conquest of the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] by joint Burawi-[[Volisanians|Volisanian]] forces in the 1560s opened the interior to colonial settlement and removed the primary indigenous political obstacle to expansion. The conquest was devastating for the Tapuya population: disease, displacement, and enslavement reduced the indigenous communities to a fraction of their pre-contact numbers. Those who survived either retreated into the deep interior, were incorporated into the colonial labour force, or assimilated into the settler population.

During the late 16th and 17th centuries, Lacashe developed into the centre of the Burawi Abyalan plantation economy. The province's fertile soils and favourable climate supported the cultivation of cotton on a scale that dwarfed the agricultural output of the other Burawi Abyalan provinces. By the 1680s, under the administration of [[Yuusuf Barre]] as Administrator General, Lacashe had become the largest cotton exporter in the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] basin, supplying [[textile]] manufacturers in [[Burawa]], [[Illypnia]], and beyond.

The plantation system was organised around large estates owned by elite Burawi families who had migrated from the peninsula during the 17th century. These families — the [[House of Vitalis]], the [[House of Rooble]], the [[House of Gurey]], and the [[House of Constans]] among them — accumulated vast landholdings and exercised political and economic power disproportionate to their numbers. By the early 18th century, a small number of planter families controlled the majority of arable land in the province.

The administration of the province was headed by an '''Administrator General''' appointed by the imperial court in Samara. The Administrator General exercised civil and military authority, supervised the colonial bureaucracy, and reported directly to the crown. [[Cusmaan Dheere]] (tenure 1624–1637) is credited with consolidating the plantation system and establishing the legal framework for land tenure. [[Yuusuf Barre]] (tenure 1685–1691) oversaw the period of peak growth in cotton exports. [[Liban Xirsi]] (tenure 1700–1709) was the last Administrator General to serve before the [[Burawi Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709.

=== Autonomous Republic (1709–1789) ===

{{main|Burawi Imperial Split}}

The [[Burawi Imperial Split]] of 1709 divided the Burawi state into two kingdoms: the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] (east) and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] (west). The three provinces of [[New Burawa]] were distributed between the successor states. [[Lacashe]] remained with the Kingdom of Burawa, while [[Bindorama]] was assigned to Galwa (provoking a civil war that led to the creation of [[Asikyira]]) and [[Arakwaya]] (the [[Hesperian League]]) formalised its already effective independence.

The settlement of 1709 granted Lacashe a new constitutional status. Rather than remaining a directly administered colony, the province was reconstituted as an '''autonomous parliamentary republic''' within the Burawi sphere. A local parliament — the '''Parliament of Lacashe''' — was established, with membership restricted to men who owned land. The parliament legislated on internal affairs, while a '''Governor''' served as liaison with the metropolitan government in Samara. Foreign affairs, including trade policy and military commitments, remained under Burawi control.

[[Markos Gurxaan]] served as the first Governor under the new arrangement (tenure 1709–1725), overseeing the transition from direct colonial administration to provincial autonomy. The autonomous period saw the peak of Lacashe's cotton economy: by the 1750s, under Governor [[Cali Dhuux]] (tenure 1753–1778), the province reached its greatest prosperity and population.

The autonomy arrangement, however, contained a latent contradiction. The planter elite who dominated the Parliament of Lacashe had built their wealth on enslaved labour, while intellectual and religious currents in metropolitan Burawa were increasingly hostile to the institution of slavery. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]], which had historically sanctioned the enslavement of non-Christians, produced a growing number of clergy who questioned the morality of the trade. By the late 18th century, abolitionist sentiment had become a significant force in Burawi politics.

=== Independence (1789) ===
{{main|Constitutional Era of Burawa|Abolition of Slavery Act 1789}}

In 1789, the Burawi parliament passed the [[Abolition of Slavery Act 1789]], which prohibited the institution of chattel slavery throughout the kingdom and its dependencies. The act was the culmination of decades of abolitionist activism and reflected the growing influence of liberal humanitarian thought in Burawi politics.

News of the act reached [[Guledga]] within weeks. The Parliament of Lacashe, dominated by planter interests, responded immediately. Under the leadership of [[Guleed Geedi]], the planter majority filed a motion declaring the province's independence from the [[Kingdom of Burawa]]. Governor [[Jaamac Warsame]] (tenure 1785–1789), himself a planter, signed the declaration, formalising the break.

Burawa's response was constrained by political reality. King [[Nuur V]], who had signed the abolition act, faced a kingdom transitioning from a slave economy; a war to reimpose slavery would have been politically impossible. The metropolitan government, moreover, had little appetite for maintaining a distant province whose economy ran counter to the kingdom's moral direction. Burawa did not initially recognise the independent state; formal recognition came only in 1792, when Burawi merchant vessels suffered attacks by pirates operating from Lacashe waters, and the kingdom sought antipiracy cooperation.

=== Post-independence to the War of Liberation (1789–1856) ===

{{main|War of Liberation (1853–1856)|Lacashe}}

The independent '''State of Lacashe''' maintained the plantation economy and the institution of chattel slavery for nearly seven decades after independence. The planter families who had declared independence continued to dominate the state: the parliamentary system remained, with the franchise restricted to landowners, ensuring that political power remained in the hands of the slaveholding elite.

Lacashe remained one of the world's largest exporters of cotton and other cash crops throughout the early 19th century. The economy generated substantial wealth for the planter class, but the continued reliance on slave labour increasingly isolated Lacashe internationally. By the 1830s, most [[Illypnia]]n powers and major Kaftian states had abolished slavery. Embargoes and trade restrictions targeted Lacashe goods, and the state became increasingly reliant on black-market trade networks that circumvented tariffs and import bans.

Internal resistance to slavery grew throughout the period. Maroon communities — settlements of escaped slaves — formed in the remote marshlands and forest interiors beyond planter control. Slave rebellions erupted periodically, most notably in 1834 and 1847, and were suppressed with violence. By the early 1850s, revolutionary organisation among the enslaved and freedman populations had intensified, with connections to abolitionist networks in Burawa, [[Volisania]], and elsewhere.

The [[War of Liberation (1853–1856)|War of Liberation]] began in 1853 when a coalition of powers — [[Burawa]], [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], [[Asikyira]], and sympathetic Illypnian states — invaded Lacashe. The coalition proclaimed the abolition of slavery as a war aim. Internal revolutionary forces, including organised cells of enslaved and freedman combatants, rose in support of the invasion. After three years of fighting, the planter government was defeated, slavery was abolished, and Lacashe was occupied by coalition forces.

The stated motives of the coalition were humanitarian — the liberation of enslaved peoples — but economic interests also played a role. Lacashe's slave-produced cotton undercut the prices of free-labour producers elsewhere, and the black-market trade that sustained the Lacashe economy operated outside the tariff regimes of the major powers. The war, while framed as a noble cause, also served to eliminate an economic competitor and bring Lacashe within the normal commercial order.

== Government and administration ==

=== Pre-1709: Administrator General ===

During the colonial period (1516–1709), Lacashe was administered by an '''Administrator General''' appointed by the imperial court in [[Samara]]. The Administrator General exercised civil and military authority within the province, supervised the colonial bureaucracy, and reported to the crown through the colonial ministry. The position carried broad discretionary powers, reflecting the distance from metropolitan oversight and the need for rapid decision-making in a colonial environment.

The Administrator General was supported by a civil service organised along lines similar to the peninsular provincial administration. Magistrates adjudicated disputes, customs officials collected duties, and land registrars tracked property transfers. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] operated a parallel ecclesiastical structure, with parishes, schools, and missions under the authority of the colonial bishopric.

