== Economy ==

[[File:Lacashe_plantation_1650.jpg|thumb|250px|Cotton plantation in Lacashe, worked by enslaved Kaftian labourers]]

The economy of Lacashe was based primarily on [[plantation agriculture]] worked by enslaved [[Kaftia]]n labourers. The province's fertile coastal lowlands, tropical climate, and reliable rainfall supported the cultivation of cash crops for export. [[Cotton]] was the dominant crop by the 18th century, making Lacashe the largest cotton exporter in the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] basin. Smaller quantities of [[tobacco]], [[sugar]], and [[indigo]] were also cultivated.

=== Cotton production ===

The shift toward cotton as the dominant crop began in the mid-17th century and accelerated through the 18th century. The rise of textile manufacturing in [[Illypnia]] and metropolitan [[Burawa]] created sustained demand for raw cotton, and Lacashe's climate proved ideal for cultivation. By the 1750s, cotton constituted approximately 80% of the province's export value.

Cotton cultivation was organised around large plantations, typically ranging from 500 to 5,000 acres, owned by the planter elite. The [[House of Vitalis]] controlled the largest holdings, followed by the [[House of Rooble]], the [[House of Gurey]], and the [[House of Constans]]. These families accumulated land through purchase, marriage, and foreclosure, concentrating ownership in a small number of hands. By the late 18th century, fewer than fifty families controlled approximately 70% of the province's arable land.

=== Trade networks ===

{{main|Kaftian slave trade}}

Lacashe was integrated into a [[triangle trade]] connecting [[Kaftia]], [[Abyala]], and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world. Enslaved persons were transported from the [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] coastal forts and peninsular ports to the Abyalan colonies. Colonial goods — primarily cotton — were shipped to Burawi and Illypnian markets. Manufactured goods returned to Kaftia, completing the cycle.

In addition to the triangular route, Lacashe maintained direct trade with [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], and other [[Aethiopian Ocean]] ports. The autonomous period (1709–1789) saw an expansion of Lacashe's commercial relationships, with planter-owned vessels calling at ports throughout the Atlantic basin. This direct trade reduced dependence on metropolitan Burawi merchants and contributed to the economic independence that made de facto political separation feasible.

== Demographics ==

{{main|Kaftian slave trade}}

The population of Lacashe was structured by the institution of slavery and the clan-based social hierarchy of the planter elite. Demographic records from the colonial period are incomplete, but estimates based on tax rolls, parish registers, and export figures suggest the following composition by the 1780s:

